  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Maryland has $24.7B in federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date. The state recorded 69,134 awards with an average award size of $357K. Obligations fell 37.7% year over year. The Department of Defense obligated $9.9B, making it the largest buyer in the state for the period. The Department of Health and Human Services followed with $4.8B. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences and Engineering Services each account for $2.7B in obligations, and Computer Systems Design Services accounts for $2.6B. THE JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY APPLIED PHYSICS LABORATORY LLC is the top vendor with $1.3B in obligations, and ORANO FEDERAL SERVICES LLC and AMERICAN CENTRIFUGE OPERATING, LLC each have $900M. Year-to-date totals are partial, and FY26 figures reflect fiscal year to date obligations only.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove the change in federal contracting in Maryland?",
        "body": "Maryland obligations fell 37.7% year over year. The decline follows FY25 obligations of $39.7B compared with FY26 obligations of $24.7B. The Department of Defense remains the largest buyer with $9.9B, but other large agencies such as the Department of Health and Human Services at $4.8B and the Department of Energy at $1.9B also shape the totals. The concentration of large vendors and high-dollar industries influences the year-to-date sum."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate FY2026 spending?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $9.9B, the largest single agency amount. THE JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY APPLIED PHYSICS LABORATORY LLC is the largest vendor with $1.3B, followed by ORANO FEDERAL SERVICES LLC at $900M and AMERICAN CENTRIFUGE OPERATING, LLC at $900M. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences and Engineering Services each account for $2.7B, and Computer Systems Design Services accounts for $2.6B."
      }
    ]
  },
